## 1. Introduction
Photocatalytic hydrogen production (PHP) based on semiconductors has attracted widespread attention in developing promising methods for converting solar energy into chemical fuels for energy storage and consumption [1–3]. Research shows that the performance of photocatalytic materials based on intrinsic semiconductor catalyst is improved mainly in three steeps: light absorption, migration and separation of photo-generated carrier, and redox reaction at photo-catalysts surface[4]. The separation of photogenerated charge and hole plays an important role in among. Hence, various strategies for improvement have been proposed: constructing heterojunction, surface modification, defect engineering, loading cocatalysts and so on [5,6]. More and more attention has been paid to load cocatalysts, which spatial cocatalyst modification is the most valid means [7]. By constructing a ternary hollow core–shell structure catalyst, the oxidation and reduction sites can be separated in physical space, which could accelerate oriented separation of electrons and holes [8]. Most importantly, due to its unique spatial configuration and the distribution of positive and negative charges at the interface of oxidation and reduction, which could promote the formation of surface polarization [9,10]. In recent years, surface polarization in heterostructure hybrids of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) catalyst has been studied extensively. Electrons can be collected to some extent and further effectively promote the separation of carriers due to surface polarization [11]. However, there are few reports about the mechanisms of photoexcited electrons storage and transfer on MOFs-based catalyst.
The photoexcited electrons transferred to the surface reduction area can effectively participate in proton reduction reaction, which is the key to the performance of hydrogen evolution. Therefore, when the charge-holes are effectively separated, how to improve the affinity of the reduction area for electrons is a top priority. Some strategies have been suggested, for examples, doping dual sites, regulating the coordination structure and building an electronic buffer area [12,13]. The establishment of electronic buffer area which can store photogenerated electrons effectively has attracted extensive attention [14]. This is because the photoexcited electrons can migrate more efficiently to the active site of PHP. Therefore, we can assume that the buffer area can be formed in some specific MOF-based catalyst with a ternary hollow core–shell structure due to the presence of surface polarization phenomena, so that photogenerated electrons can be temporarily stored to support the requirement call from the surface active-sites. However, If the supply of active sites is insufficient, even a large number of electrons stored in the buffer area cannot obtain enough active sites, resulting in electron annihilation. So, we can further assume that for PHP, ensuring that the active center is dispersed and rich may be extremely important for the formation of smooth electron transfer channels from the electron libraries to the active sites.
MOFs have been widely used in photocatalytic materials in recent years due to their unique structure, such as metal centers and organic ligands coexisting, large specific surface area and flexible design [15]. For the intrinsic characteristics of MOFs, on the one hand, the metal center can be used as the active sites, such as forming dispersed metalloid quantum dots (QDs) to enhance the reaction sites [16]. On the other hand, the organic ligands rich in carbon and nitrogen becomes buffer area around active sites to store electron called as electron library [17]. It’s obvious that the stronger the storage capacity of the electron library, the higher the utilization rate of electrons transferred to the active-sites. Hence, how to improve the fitness related the storage capacity of the electronic library and the number of surfaces active-sites becomes fantastic urgent matter. A number of researches show that microelectronic state of organic ligands has a profound influence on the properties of MOFs materials such as electronics library and electronic transport [18,19]. Microelectronic states could be regulated by electron-withdrawing and electron-giving groups [20,21] resulting in the storage capacity of the electron library in MOFs which could induce the speed of electron transfer between catalytic components.
In this work, we provide a more in-depth study on the synthesis of ternary hollow core–shell photocatalysts with oxidation and reduction components by template method self-assembled. The spatial separation of oxidation and reduction components gives it the characteristics of surface polarization, which facilitates electron transport to reduction area. The Co-based metal–organic frameworks (Co-MOF) containing imidazole with methyl or imidazole with benzene ring could be used as an electron library to store electrons for meeting the requirements of the CoP QDs active-sites. Compared with the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN $(23.69\ \mathrm{mmol/h/g})$ with electron-withdrawing benzene-imidazole functional groups CN layers and $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ -CoP QDs $\mathrm{11.08~mmol/}$ $\mathbf{h}/\mathbf{g},$ without imidazole CN layers), the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN $(35.31~\mathrm{\mmol/h/g}$ , with electron-giving methyl-imidazole functional groups CN layers) has more excellent hydrogen evolution performance. This can be interpreted as that when photogenerated electrons are transferred to the reduction area on the surface of ternary hollow core–shell photocatalysts, the photogenerated electrons can be effectively stored in electron libraries with different storage capacities due to the existence of polarization phenomenon, waiting for the call of CoP QDs as active sites. Whereas the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN with abundant CoP QDs as active sites, thus PHP is enhanced because photogenerated electrons can combine with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ smoothly along the electron channel from the electron library to the active center.
## 2. Experiment section
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
Tetraethyl orthosilicate (AR, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\geq28.4\%\$ , ammonia monohydrate $\mathrm{(NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $25\mathrm{-}28\%$ , ethyl alcohol (AR, $99.7\%$ , manganese acetate tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(Mn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ AR, $99\%$ , trisodium citrate dihydrate $(C_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{Na}_{3}0_{7},$ , AR, $99.0\%$ ), cadmium chloride hemi $(\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}.2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $98\%$ ), thiourea $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}S$ , AR, $99.0\%$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, AR, $96.0\%$ ), cobaltous nitrate hexahydrate (Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $98.5\%$ ), 2-methylimidazole $\mathrm{\C_{4}H_{6}N_{2}}$ AR, $98\%$ ), methanol ( $\mathrm{CH}_{3}{\mathrm{OH}}$ , AR, $99.5\%$ ), benzimidazole ( $:C_{7}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , AR, $98.5\%$ ), cobaltous hydroxide $(\mathsf{C o}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ , AR, $99.9\%$ and Sodium hypophosphite $(\mathsf{N a H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{2}.\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR, $99.0\%$ ) were purchased from Macklin reagent Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Synthesis of catalysts
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of $S i O_{2}$
$\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres templates were synthesized by Stöber method. And the specific steps were following: Put $456~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethyl alcohol into $45~\mathrm{mL}$ tetraethyl orthosilicate and stir the mixture for a few minutes. Then the above mixed solution was added a solution of $283~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethyl alcohol and $70\mathrm{mL}28\%\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ mixed with $147~\mathrm{mL}$ Deionized water (DI), and stirred at room temperature $(25\pm2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was washing 2–3 times with water and ethyl alcohol sequentially.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of $S i O_{2}@M n O x$
Firstly, the $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ as-prepared $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ were mixed with $70~\mathrm{mL}$ DI and sonicated for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . After that $80~\mathrm{mg~Mn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ were put in the above solution, stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Then, the obtained mixture was transferred to Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and held $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Thirdly, the powder obtained above was calcined in a tubular furnace $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of $S i O_{2}@M n O x@C d S$
The $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ calcined powder mixing with ${80}~\mathrm{{mL}}$ DI were sonicated for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the mixture solution added $500~\mathrm{mg}~{\mathsf{C}}_{6}{\mathsf{H}}_{5^{-}}$ $\mathsf{N a}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{7}$ and $400\mathrm{\mg\CdCl_{2}{\cdot}2.5H_{2}O}$ was stirred for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ . Finally, putting into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{0}$ and 200 mg $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}S$ held the oil bath $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting products were collected.
## 2.2.4. Synthesis of MnOx $@$ CdS (Mn@Cd)
$500~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{MnOx}@\mathrm{CdS}$ powder obtained above was dissolved into $50~\mathrm{mL}~2.5~\mathrm{M}~\mathrm{NaOH}$ , and then transferred to Teflonlined stainless-steel autoclave for at $90\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ after ultrasound 30 min. Finally, the $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ powder was obtained by centrifugation and dried.
2.2.5. Synthesis of Co-MOF (CoP QDs/MCN and CoP QDs/BCN) and Co $(O H)_{2}$ derived CoP QDs
CoP QDs/MCN before high temperature phosphating: $300{\mathrm{~mg}}C_{0}$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $330~\mathrm{\mg}~\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}N_{2}}$ were respectively placed in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, stirred for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to mixing uniformity. Then, the two solutions were mixed together and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Then rested to stand at room temperature $(25\pm2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the powder was washed via centrifugation and dried.
CoP QDs/MCN: $50~\mathrm{mg}$ the above powder were placed at the downstream of a quartz reactor, and $250\ \mathrm{mg\NaH_{2}P O_{2}.H_{2}O}$ were placed at the upstream of the quartz reactor as phosphorus source. Then, they were calcined for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ at $350~\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ in inert gases atmosphere.
CoP QDs/BCN before high temperature phosphating: $230{\mathrm{~mg}}$ benzimidazole and 280 mg $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ DI with stirring for $15\mathrm{min}$ , and then put into 5 mL $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}.}\end{array}$ - O. The resulting solution was placed to rest at room temperature $(25\pm2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ in the dark, then washed with DI for several times and dried in vacuum.
CoP QDs/BCN: The experiment process was the same as CoP QDs/MCN. It just replaced the precursors as CoP QDs/BCN before high temperature phosphating.
CoP QDs: The experiment process was the same as CoP $\mathsf{Q D s}/$ MCN. It just replaced the precursors as ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ .
2.2.6. Synthesis of MnOx@CdS-CoP QDs/MCN (Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN), MnOx@CdS-CoP QDs/BCN (Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN) and MnOx@CdSCoP QDs (Mn@Cd-CoP QDs)
Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN: The $100~\mathrm{mg}$ of the above prepared $\mathrm{Mn}@{\mathsf{C}}\mathrm{d}$ were dispersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ DI with stirred and ultrasound for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then CoP QDs/MCN was added into the solution, ultrasound and stirred for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
The preparation method of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN and Mn@CdCoP QDs was the same as above the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN.
