## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Materials characterization
As illustrated in (Fig. 1a), the primarily synthesis process for ternary hollow core–shell structure with template method is exhibited in (Fig. 1a). And the hollow core–shell structure is capped with Co-MOF which the carbon and nitrogen (CN) layer locked CoP QDs via electrostatic adsorption on the surface of core–shell catalyst to form a thin-shell. The Zeta analysis that CdS has a negative potential $(-0.116~\mathrm{mV})$ and Co-MOF has a positive potential $(0.262~\mathrm{mV})$ in Table S1, so the electrostatic adsorption self-assembly method is possible.
As illustrated in (Fig. 1b), X-ray diffraction (XRD) was performed to explain the crystal of the as-prepared samples. The primary sharp peaks relevant to hexagonal CdS phase (JCPDS, No.41– 1049) [22]. The major peaks of CdS-based samples are located in (100), (002), (101), (110) and (112) planes. The Co-MOF which was phosphating by high temperature from different precursors is shown in (Fig. 1c) [23]. The peak of the amorphous CN layer before $20^{\circ}$ could be clearly observed, and we could clearly see that (0 1 1), (1 1 1), (1 1 2), (2 1 1) and (3 0 1) planes attribute to the characteristic crystal planes of CoP QDs [24,25]. However, in contrast of the pristine CdS, there are weak peaks of CoP QDs, because a small number of Co-MOF is loaded on $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ and CoP QDs is locked in the CN layer. Moreover, on peaks of the MnOx is discovered. The reason might be attributed to the low amount and inside the hollow core–shell structure.
The sunflower Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN composite was prepared by using $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres as sacrificial templates [26]. The morphologies of the synthetic catalysts were characterized through transmission electron microscope (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM). As illustrated in (Fig S1a), the original CdS is evenly loaded on the hollow sphere, and MnOx is successfully and uniformly loaded on the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ in (Fig S1b). The morphologies of $\mathrm{Mn}@{\mathsf{C}}\mathrm{d}$ and Cd-CoP QDs/BCN are shown in (Fig S1c and S1d). Hence, we could conclude that the template method has successfully prepared the desired structure. The CoMOF which CoP QDs are locked in CN layers derived from different precursors is displayed in (Fig. 2a-2b and Fig S1e-1f). As shown in (Fig. 2a and 2b), the Co-MOF derived from methyl-imidazole and benzene-imidazole are modified with about $5\ \mathrm{nm}\ C\mathrm{oP}$ QDs, which are uniformly and dispersedly locked in the CN layers. The lattice fringes with an interplanar spacing of $0.185~\mathrm{{nm}}$ corresponding to the (2 1 1) planes of CoP QDs [23]. In contrast, a larger number of CoP QDs derived from ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ are clustered together because of no CN layer in (Fig. 2c and Fig S1g). Therefore, the CN layer could be considered as the carrier of dispersed active-sites CoP QDs. To further proved that MnOx is located on the inner of hollow core– shell and Co-MOF is located on the outer of hollow core–shell in (Fig. 2d). The sunflower Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN with a hollow spherical diameter of about $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a shell thickness of about $50\mathrm{nm}$ are shown in (Fig. 2d and Fig S1g). The spacing of $0.358~\mathrm{nm}$ can be attributed to the (100) atomic planes of CdS and the lattice fringes with spacing of $0.245~\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.185\mathrm{nm}$ respectively correspond to the (111) and (211) plane of CoP QDs in (Fig. 2e) [22,23], which consistent with the XRD results. The Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN have the same morphological characteristics as Mn@Cd-CoP $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{QDs}}/$ MCN in (Fig. 2f), which has ultra-thin shell CN layer. (Fig. 2g) is TEM of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs, which is not clearly sunflower due to the lack of CN layer encapsulation. The energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is shown (Fig. 2h), and the distribution of various atoms in the hollow core–shell structure is revealed in (Fig. 2i-2q). All the elements could be clearly observed and spread out in a spherical form. The scanning electron microscope (SEM) images presented in Fig S2. All samples are about $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ spherical, indicating the successful preparation of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ template method in (Fig S2a-2f) and the sphericity does not change during the synthesis process, which indicated that the physical morphology stability is heavy fantastic. The CoP QDs/MCN, CoP QDs/BCN and CoP QDs are shown in (Fig $S2\mathrm{g}{\cdot}S2\mathrm{i}{\cdot}$ ). Compared with the catalysts morphology before high temperature phosphating shown in (Fig S2j-2 k), we could conclude that high temperature phosphating may change the morphology of the precursor of Co-MOF.
As shown in (Fig. 3a), the chemical structures of CoP QDs/MCN, CoP QDs/BCN and CoP QDs were characterized by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR). From the spectra, the characteristic vibration at $1560{-}1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bending vibrations [23]. In the spectra of the CoP QDs/MCN and CoP QDs/BCN, the imidazole characteristic absorption bands at $1500{-}1550\quad\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and 1000– $1200~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the stretching vibration of the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ , respectively [27]. The stretching vibration of benzene ring vibration for the spectra of the CoP QDs/BCN are located at $1200{-}1500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}[28]$ . What is noteworthy is that Co-N stretching vibrations are shown in $450{-}750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [29]. Interestingly, because the CoP QDs has no CN layer of imidazole, it does not contain the characteristic peaks mentioned above. Hence, we can obtain different characteristics of the reduction area. Additionally, as illustrated in Fig S3a, the band at 1020, 1380 and $1647~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the characteristic absorptions of CdS, which are observed in all the samples [30]. All the above characteristics can be concluded the successful preparation of different ternary hollow core–shell structures.
The structure of the graphitization degree of reduction area including CoP QDs/MCN, CoP QDs/BCN and CoP QDs were examined by Raman spectroscopy in (Fig. 3b). It has been detailly documented that the D band should be induced by the vacancy at the $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ defect sites in the Co-MOF [31], while the G band could be attributed to the degree of graphitization in the Co-MOF [32]. The intensity D and G of CoP QDs/MCN is higher than CoP QDs/ BCN. Hence, the CoP QDs/MCN has a greater degree of defect which could be as library of lone pairs of electrons [33], and higher electronic transmission capacity. In terms of CoP QDs, 671 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , $514~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $470~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $189~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is Co-oxidation, and CoP QDs is located in $269~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}[34]$ . No D-band and G-band are observed in CoP QDs, which proves that there are no CN layers. As shown in (Fig S3b), the peaks of $302~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $604~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the CdS, and all samples have characteristic peaks of CdS.
UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) spectra was applied for ulterior detection of optical properties of all samples in (Fig. 4a). And the bandgap energies are calculated based on Tauc plots [35] in Table S2. It can be seen that the light absorption capacity is significantly enhanced when the oxidation area of MnOx and reduction area which Co-MOF consist CN layers locked CoP QDs are loaded. The CdS shows visible light absorption edge at $542\mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to the bandgap (Eg) of 2.267 eV. When oxidation and reduction area are loaded, the visible light absorption edge goes from $542~\mathrm{nm}$ to $609~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Interestingly, the light absorption capacity of the different Co-MOF is not the same. The absorption capacity of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is slightly larger than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN. Because the electron-giving capacity of methyl is greater than benzene ring, which makes the imidazole with methyl group more electron density than the imidazole with benzene ring, thus changing the absorption capacity of light[36]. However, Mn@Cd-CoP QDs is the lowest of the ternary catalysts, because CoP QD of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs does not have an electronrich imidazole ring CN layers. Mott–Schottky (M S) plots of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN, and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN and $\mathrm{Mn@Cd-}$ CoP QDs could conclude all of them are $n$ -type catalysis with a flat-band potential correlated to CBM shown in (Fig. 4b) [37], the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is the largest CBM.
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to reveal the element components and surface chemical valences of Mn@CdCoP QDs/MCN. The full survey spectra of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is shown in (Fig. 5a), which indicates the presence of Mn, O, Cd, S, Co,
P, C and N elements. (Fig S4a) reveals the Cd 3d, the peaks at 411.81 eV and $405.07\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to the Cd ${\bf d}_{3/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ [38], respectively. And when MnOx is loaded, the peaks move to the direction of low binding energy, indicating a modulated local electronic of Cd in the presence of cocatalysts, which may be MnOx acted as a hole collector [22]. As shown in (Fig. 5b), when the CoMOF and CoP QDs are anchored, the peaks of Mn@Cd-CoP $\mathsf{Q D s}/$ MCN $(0.45\mathrm{eV})$ move the most towards the direction of high binding energy than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN $(0.30\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$
QDs $(0.22{\ \mathrm{eV}})$ , indicating that the most electrons are transferred from Cd [39]. It may be because electron-giving methylimidazole attracts the most electrons of transferred between catalysts components, which makes the largest storage capacity of electron library of CoP QDs/MCN, causing more outperformance transmission capacity of the electron in CoP QDs/MCN. But due to the absence of the CN layer in CoP QDs, the photogenerated electron cannot be efficiently transmitted to the active site on the surface. As shown in (Fig S4b), the peaks at $161.45\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $162.66~\mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to the $\textsf{S}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ and $S\ 2\mathsf{p}\ \%$ [38]. In Fig S4c and S4d, there are peaks of Mn and O belonging to the MnOx which located in inner surface of the shell-core[26], and when CoP QDs/MCN is loaded on $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ , the binding energy goes toward high, indicating electron cloud around density of Mn decrease. It could be that the electrons around $\mathtt{M n O x}$ have moved to CoP QDs/MCN, leaving more holes in MnOx. As shown in (Fig. 5c-5e), there are peaks of Co 2p in the sample, and the $778.84\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $793.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ in (Fig. 5c) [40]. When different Co-MOF are anchored, all the peaks are slightly shift, suggesting the changes in surface electron density. And the peak of $\mathrm{Mn}@{\mathsf{C d-}}$ CoP QDs/MCN $(0.82\mathrm{eV})$ move most seriously towards the direction of low binding energy than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN (0.55 eV) and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs $(0.26\mathrm{eV})$ , which may ascribe to fastest and most efficiently electrons migration in the Co-MOF containing electrongiving methyl. Because compared with electron-withdrawing benzene ring, the methyl is rich in electron state, which makes rich electron state on imidazole ring which could attracted more electrons to transfer between catalyst components and cause the largest electronic library, and then affects the carriage of electrons to actives CoP QDs. Since the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs does not provide the electronic library, it has the least influence. As shown in (Fig $S4\mathrm{e}{\mathrm{-}}S4\mathrm{g})$ ), there are the P peaks of three kinds of ternary hollow core–shell photocatalysts. In (Fig S4e) the peaks of $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ are 129.32 eV $(\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2})$ and 130.41 eV $(\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ in Co-P[41], while the peak at 134.02 eV is the oxidized P because of the inevitable surface oxidation[42]. The binding energy of $\mathtt{C o}\ 2\mathtt{p}$ is higher than the reported metallic Co $(778.1~\mathrm{~eV})$ ; Meanwhile, the binding energy of $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ is lower than elemental P $(130.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , suggesting the existence of electron transfer between Co and P, because Co has a small positive charge and $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ has a small negative charge [43]. Wherein Co is active centers and the P is the hydride and proton acceptor, promoting the rate of hydrogen production by photoreduction[44,45]. The C 1 s XPS spectrum reveals the characteristic CAC, CAN and $N-C=N$ species of Co-MOF in (Fig. 5f). And in the (Fig. 5g) the N 1 s XPS spectrum proves that the existences of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{N-C}}$ , Co-N, graphitic N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -oxide and $\pi$ excitations are presented at binding energies of 398.32 eV, 399.68 eV, 401.11 eV, 402.37 eV and $404.82\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively[46–49].
## 3.2. Photocatalytic $H_{2}$ performance and stability test
The hydrogen evolution performance of samples was conducted in $10\%$ vol lactic acid aqueous solution under irradiation with a 300 W Xe lamp (equipped with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter). All photocatalytic hydrogen evolution tests were performed three times under exactly the same experimental conditions. The experimental value is the average of the three repeated measurements and its standard deviation is shown by the error bar for each data point in the figure. As shown in (Fig. 6a), the hydrogen yield of CdS is only $0.9244\mathrm{\mmol/h/g}$ , however, after loading the MnOx hole collector $(\mathbb{M}\mathbb{n}@\mathbb{C}\mathbb{d}),$ , the hydrogen yield is slightly increased to $1.043\mathrm{mmol/h/g}$ . Because MnOx can capture some holes, increasing the concentration of photoreduction carriers. But surface-active sites of CdS are not enough, hydrogen production is not significantly increased. Interestingly, when the Co-MOF is anchored, hydrogen production is significantly increased. This is because the reduction area of Co-MOF could act as library of lone pairs of electrons and wait for demand call of surface active-sites CoP QDs to improve the electron utilization. Therefore, the hydrogen production of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN reaches $35.31\ \mathrm{mmol/h/g}$ and 23.69 mmol $/\mathrm{h}/\mathrm{g},$ which is approximately 38 times and 26 times higher than CdS, respectively. And the $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$ QDs without Co-MOF only reaches $11.08\ \mathrm{mmol}\ ,$ $/$ $\mathbf{h}/\mathbf{g},$ because the lack of electronic library saves the emergent electron causing quenching a large number of emergent electrons. As shown in (Fig S5a), CoP QDs/MCN with different contents is loaded, and it can be clearly seen that $7\%$ CoP QDs/MCN is the optimal content. Hydrogen production is compared with previous work shown in Table S3. The apparent quantum yield (AQY) of CdS, $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN at different single wavelengths are shown (Fig. 6b). With the UV-DRS spectrum from $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $520~\mathrm{nm}$ , the AQE gradually decreased, suggesting that the light absorption capacity of sample plays a significant role in the quantum yield [3,50]. By compared with samples, the AQE of $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathsf{C d-C o P\ Q D s}/\$ MCN is higher than $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ and CdS. As can be seen in (Fig. 6c), after the stability test, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production of $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}\ \mathrm{QDs}/\$ MCN didn’t significantly decrease, because the amount of sacrificial agent in the reaction was consumed and the catalyst components may fall off[51,52]. Besides, the XRD and TEM of $\mathrm{Mn@Cd-}$ CoP QDs/MCN obtained after the stability test is virtually identical to the before in (Fig S5b-S5d). However, the crystal plane (011) in XRD disappeared, which may be because the crystal plane (011) is unstable under self-assembly
## 3.3. Photocatalytic mechanism
The charge carriers behaviors could be a significant index to evaluate the photocatalysis. Hence, the charge carriers’ kinetics of sample was further explored by the electro-chemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), the transient photocurrent density-time response curves (i-t), the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectrum and time-resolved photoluminescence (TR-PL) spectrum. As depicted in (Fig. 7a), the interfacial charge transfer dynamics of all the samples was explored by EIS. It obviously observed that the EIS curves of all samples are about half circles. The semicircular Nyquist plots shows the charge transfer process [8,53]. It intuitively observed that the radius goes from large to small: Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN, Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN and $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$ QDs, indicating that the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN has the fastest charge transfer. Owing to the Co-MOF with CN layer [54], which causing surface polarizations under dark condition accelerates electrons transfer providing electron library, the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN possessed small charge transfer resistance. Compared Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN with Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN, the CN layer containing methyl has a larger electron cloud on the imidazole ring to accelerate electron transport in catalytic components, which makes have more electrons in electronic library. As shown in (Fig. 7b), $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ has the highest photocurrent density. However, in terms of combined hydrogen production (Fig. 6a), the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production of $\mathrm{Mn}@{\mathsf{C}}\mathrm{d}$ is much lower than the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN, indicating that CoP QDs is very necessary as active-sites, which could improve the utilization rate of photogenerated electrons. Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN has the highest photocurrent except for $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ . This result suggests that MnOx and Co-MOF provide the most facilitated charge-carrier separation and the best light response[55]. Furthermore, studying the charge availability and transfer rate are essential to improve photocatalysis performance, because charge carriers’ kinetics is linking light absorption with catalysis reaction and regulates efficiency. The steady -state and time-resolved PL measurement are essential characterizations for the charge availability and transfer rate, exploring catalytic processes from kinetics [56]. As displayed (Fig. 7c), at the excitation wavelength of $325\mathrm{nm}$ , CdS shows a wide peak at about $550\mathrm{nm}$ , which is due to the recombination of radiative electron holes on the surface. The radiation intensity of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is the lowest among all the samples, indicating during the excitation process the efficient separation of electron holes, due to loading of oxidation and reduction area forming surface polarizations to build electron library accelerating electron transport to the surface CoP QDs active-sites. This process can be confirmed by time-resolved PL (TRPL). As depicted in (Fig. 7d), the TRPL decay curves can be mathematically fitted by the biexponential function Eq.(1):
$I(t)=I_{0}+A_{1}\mathbf{exp}(-t/\tau_{1})+A_{2}\mathbf{exp}(-t/\tau_{2})\backprime$ Where $\mathrm{I}_{0}$ is the baseline correction value; $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ are lifetimes (ns) for radiative and nonradiative recombination processes, respectively. And $\mathsf{A}_{1}$ and $\mathsf{A}_{2}$ are corresponding amplitudes. In addition, the average lifetime $(\uptau_{\mathrm{a}})$ can be calculated by the equation Eq.(2):
$$
{\tau_{a}}=({A_{1}}{\tau_{1}}^{2}+{A_{2}}{\tau_{2}}^{2})/({A_{1}}\tau_{1}+{A_{2}}\tau_{2})
$$
The lifetime calculation results are shown in Table 1. And $\tau_{1}$ represents the radiative recombination of electrons-holes in the fluorescence. Among the as-prepared samples, Mn@Cd-CoP $\mathsf{Q D}\mathsf{s}/$ MCN has a relatively smallest $\tau_{1}$ about 1.330 ns than $\operatorname{Mn@Cd-}$ CoP QDs/BCN $(1.500~\mathrm{ns})$ and $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$ QDs $(1.450~\mathrm{ns})$ , the decrease of $\tau_{1}$ indicates the decrease of recombination at the band-to-band [55], indicating the important for building electron library. Meanwhile, $\tau_{2}$ represents the lifetime in nonradiative energy decay processes. The $\tau_{2}$ of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN (9.420 ns) is obviously lowest than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN (18.05 ns) and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs (21.79 ns), which $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ -CoP QDs/MCN possesses the most efficient nonradiative decay pathway and the highest attenuated transition rate [57]. And the average lifetime $\left(\tau_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN about 8.07 ns is smallest. Due to modification with oxidation area MnOx and reduction area Co-MOF, the existence of electron library is caused by surface polarization and high performance active-site CoP QDs are exposed on outer surface of hollow core–shell, which could increase the photoexcited charge orientated transfer[58]. For quantification of the charge transfer kinetics, the separation rate of photogenerated charge can be further evaluated by electron transfer rate constant $(\mathsf{K}_{\mathtt{E T}})$ and electron injection efficiency $(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\mathrm{ET}})$ , the equation is as follows Eq. (3):
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{KET}=\frac{1}{\tau_{a\nu e}(c o m p o s i t e)}-\frac{1}{\tau_{a\nu e}(C d S)}}\\ &{\eta\mathrm{ET}=1-\frac{\tau_{a\nu e}(c o m p o s i t e)}{\tau_{a\nu e}(C d S)}}\end{array}
$$
As shown above, the photogenerated carriers have the fastest charge transfer rate $(\mathsf{K}_{\mathtt{E T}})$ and higher charge injection efficiency $(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\mathrm{ET}})$ of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN in Table 2.. This is due to the effective separation of charge holes in space by MnOx and Co-MOF, as well as electron library and the efficient transport of electrons by the CN layer in Co-MOF. In conclusion, the above proves that MnOx as a hole catcher and Co-MOF as an electron collector carry out efficient separation in space, making the electron hole move oriented to cause surface polarizations which is beneficial to the formation of electron library. And the CN layer in Co-MOF plays an important role in electronic library and efficient transport and utilization electron, which the Co-MOF with methyl is more likely to transfer than Co-MOF with benzene ring, because the methyl making the imidazole ring electron-rich could accelerate the transfer between catalytic components.
In order to further dynamic detection of electron and hole transfer direction and explore the influence of Co-MOF with methyl and benzimidazole on surface polarization forming electron library, we performed in-situ surface photovoltage images acquired with a Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) in Atomic Force Microscope (AFM) system to test on $\mathsf{M n@C d-C o P~Q D s/M C N}$ and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN and calculated their work function. As shown in (Fig. 8a-d and 8f-i) are Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN of AFM and KPFM under dark and light conditions, respectively.
We explore different influence of Co-MOF with methylimidazole or benzene-imidazole on surface polarization forming electron library, as shown in (Fig. 8a-d and 8f-i). In (Fig. 8a-b) and (Fig. 8f-g), they represent morphology and surface charge distribution of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN under dark condition, respectively. And it could represent the degree of surface polarization [11,59]. Compared (Fig. 8b) with (Fig. 8g), we could obviously see that the surface polarization of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is more serious than that of $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$ QDs/BCN. Because the different ability of the reduction area, the electron driving in the oxidation and reduction area is different under dark condition, which leads to the different surface polarization phenomenon. Surface polarization can effectively balance the Coulomb force between electron-holes and accelerate electron transfer, so that when light radiates, a large number of excited electrons are transferred to the reduction area. As shown in (Fig. 8d and Fig. 8i), we could obviously sure that Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN has more electron on the surface than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN under simulated sunlight illumination. This is because under conditions of serious surface polarization, when light radiates, more electrons are transferred to the electron library for storage, resulting in the storage capacity of CoP QDs/MCN electron library bigger. All the reason could ascribe the microelectronic states regulating by electron-giving methyl-imidazole and electron-withdrawing benzene-imidazole. Compared with Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN, the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN has a small surface potential (SP) under dark condition and under simulated sunlight illumination in Table 3. Hence the surface polarization of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN is larger than Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN, and the library of electrons of methyl-imidazole is greater than benzene-imidazole in Co-MOF. As shown in (Fig. 8e and 8j), there are the change of surface potential profiles along the arrow direction. And it shows the photoelectron overflows from the electron library under illumination, then it availably transfers to the surface active-sites of CoP QDs for hydrogen reduction. At the same time, the KPFM 3D image of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN are shown in Fig S6.
Work function (WF) is an important parameter for predicting catalytic performance of catalysts. A lower work function implies the density of states nearby the Fermi level $\left(\mathrm{E_{f}}\right)$ . And a lower energy barrier is effectively transferring electrons from catalysts to surface for hydrogen reduction. KPFM measurement was applied to the calculation of work functions (WFs) of the samples. WFs can be obtained by the equation presented below [60]:
$W_{s a m p l e}=W_{t i p}(5e V)+e^{-}(S P)$ where W and SP are the work function and surface potential, respectively.
The work function of Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN and Mn@Cd-CoP $\mathsf{Q D s/B C N}$ are shown in Table 3. As shown in (Fig. 8k and 8 l), the WF is calculated by subtracting the electron cutoff energy (Ecutoff) from the He I UPS spectra of $21.22\mathrm{eV}$ through the Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) spectra [50]. Hence, the WF of CdS and $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ are $5.220\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $5.170\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. And the WF represents the difference between the vacuum level and the Fermi level. CdS is $n$ -type semiconductor photocatalysis with a big WF $(5.220~\mathrm{eV})$ and Fermi level $(-5.220\ \mathrm{eV}$ vs. vacuum). When MnOx is placed on the internal of CdS, the WF of $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ is decreasing. This may be because the work function of MnOx is smaller than the CdS. And therefore, when they are combined, electrons flow from MnOx to CdS until reaching equilibrium [61]. In a similar way, it could be inferred that the work function of Co-MOF is lower than that of $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ . But since the work function of $\mathrm{Mn}@\mathrm{Cd-CoP}$ QDs/MCN is decreasing by more than $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ , we could think that work function of Co-MOF is the smallest. Hence, as shown in Table S4 and (Fig. 9a), when the Co-MOF is anchored on $\mathrm{Mn@Cd}$ , it will built-in electric field between Co-MOF and $\mathtt{M n@C d}$ . And it be in tune with XPS and KPFM analysis results which MnOx could be used as hole-consuming and Co-MOF could be as library of electrons.
As shown in (Fig. 9b) presents the electron and hole transfer mechanism in Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN. The electrons of CdS are excited from the VB to CB under light illumination. Due to the surface polarization and the bending of the conduction band, the electrons are stored in electrons library in CN layer of Co-MOF, and then availably transferred to the surface-active sites of CoP QDs, participating in proton reduction to produce hydrogen. At the same time, the holes transfers to MnOx on the inner surface of the hollow core–shell structure. Therefore, Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN has high efficiency of electron-hole separation and electron utilization, because the ternary hollow core–shell structure directionally separates the electron-holes and the Co-MOF on the outer core–shell structure serves as an electron library making electrons convenient and fast delivery to active-sites CoP QDs.
## 4. Conclusions
The ternary hollow core–shell structure application coated by Co-MOF is successfully fabricated for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production under light illumination, whereas electrons and holes can be directional separated to oxidation area MnOx and reduction area Co-MOF building surface polarization. Importantly, the results show that the catalytic performance of these photocatalysts is far better than that of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ . The reason can be explained that Co-MOF of CN layers with methyl or benzene can serve as electron library to wait support the requirement call from the surface active-sites CoP QDs. Further discussion shows that the electron library is significant difference in storage capacity and electron transfer rate. It attributes to the influence on the electron cloud density of imidazole ring through methyl with electron-giving ability and benzene with electron-sucking ability. Thus, Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN $(35.31\mathrm{\mmol/h/g})$ , Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/BCN $(23.69\mathrm{\mmol/h/g})$ and Mn@Cd-CoP QDs ( $11.08\ \mathrm{mmol/h/g})$ have the best catalytic performances for PHP, which is about 38 times, 26 times and 12 times higher than CdS $\mathrm{(0.9244~mmol/h/g)}$ , respectively. Whereas the Mn@Cd-CoP QDs/MCN with abundant CoP QDs as active sites, thus PHP is enhanced because photogenerated electrons can combine with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ smoothly along the electron channel from the electron library to the active center. It confirmed our previous two hypotheses that the electron library can be formed in some specific MOFbased catalyst with the ternary hollow core–shell structure due to the presence of surface polarization, and the dispersed and rich CoP QDs as active-sites is extremely important for the formation of a smooth electron transfer channel from the electron library to the active center. The problem of separation and utilization of photoexcited electrons can be solved by forming smooth electron transfer channels from electron libraries to quantum dots as active sites.
